      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Terraform for Auth0\nThe Auth0 provider lets Terraform\nconfigure an Auth0 tenant through\nthe Auth0 Management API\n([provider docs](https://github.co\nm/auth0/terraform-provider-auth0/b\nlob/v1.63.0/docs/index.md#L9-L14))\n. Auth0 maintains it under the\nsource address `auth0/auth0`.\nAuth0 is a hosted sign-in service;\nthe [authentication map](../../aut\nhentication/authentication.md#auth\n0-auth0) explains what it does and\nhow OAuth works. The [Terraform\nmap](../terraform.md) explains\nTerraform itself.\n\nThe unit is one tenant. A tenant\nis one isolated Auth0 space with\nits own users, applications and\nsettings. One provider\nconfiguration manages exactly one\ntenant, and the API cannot create\ntenants, so someone creates each\ntenant by hand first ([tenant doc]\n(https://github.com/auth0/terrafor\nm-provider-auth0/blob/v1.63.0/docs\n/resources/tenant.md#L11-L12)).\nTerraform signs in to the tenant\nas one machine-to-machine\napplication, and the scopes of\nthat application decide what\nTerraform can read and change\n([One Tenant, One Identity](#one-t\nenant-one-identity-one-tenant-one-\nidentity)).\n\nA tenant holds these objects:\n\n- Applications, also called\nclients: the apps that sign users\nin, and the programs that call\nAPIs.\n- APIs, also called resource\nservers, with their scopes.\n- Client grants, which allow a\nmachine-to-machine application to\ncall an API.\n- Connections: the places where\nusers sign in, such as a user\ndatabase or a company directory.\n- Roles, organizations, and\nactions, which are small programs\nthat run during sign-in.\n- Log streams, which send the\ntenant's logs to another system.\n- Tenant-wide settings, such as\nbranding and the sign-in pages.\n\nThe provider has three families of\nresources for these objects\n([Three Families of Resources](#th\nree-families-of-resources-three-fa\nmilies-of-resources)). The family\ndecides what an import ID looks\nlike and what a destroy does.\nThree more ideas explain most\nplans:\n\n- For links between objects, a\nplural resource owns the whole\nlist, and a singular resource adds\none item ([Plural Owns, Singular A\ndds](#plural-owns-singular-adds-pl\nural-owns-singular-adds)).\n- An action is a draft until it is\ndeployed, and it runs only when it\nis bound to a flow ([Actions Have\nThree States](#actions-have-three-\nstates-actions-have-three-states))\n.\n- The state holds client secrets,\naction secrets and\nidentity-provider secrets ([State\nIs a Secrets Store](#state-is-a-se\ncrets-store-state-is-a-secrets-sto\nre)).\n\nNow compare Aiven. Both providers\nare remote controls for a service\nthat someone else runs. In Auth0,\nhowever, people also change the\nsame tenant in its dashboard every\nday, so drift is normal.\n\nThe map has four parts. Mental\nModels explains the ideas above.\nReading an Auth0 Configuration\nsays what you will see and where\nto look first. Pitfalls lists the\nfailures that experienced users\nplan around. Good and Bad\nDecisions compares the main design\nchoices.",
      "fontSize": 18,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 22.5,
      "containerId": "node-b6aff488e8cc",
      "originalText": "Terraform for Auth0\nThe Auth0 provider lets Terraform configure an Auth0 tenant through the Auth0 Management API ([provider docs](https://github.com/auth0/terraform-provider-auth0/blob/v1.63.0/docs/index.md#L9-L14)). Auth0 maintains it under the source address `auth0/auth0`. Auth0 is a hosted sign-in service; the [authentication map](../../authentication/authentication.md#auth0-auth0) explains what it does and how OAuth works. The [Terraform map](../terraform.md) explains Terraform itself.\n\nThe unit is one tenant. A tenant is one isolated Auth0 space with its own users, applications and settings. One provider configuration manages exactly one tenant, and the API cannot create tenants, so someone creates each tenant by hand first ([tenant doc](https://github.com/auth0/terraform-provider-auth0/blob/v1.63.0/docs/resources/tenant.md#L11-L12)). Terraform signs in to the tenant as one machine-to-machine application, and the scopes of that application decide what Terraform can read and change ([One Tenant, One Identity](#one-tenant-one-identity-one-tenant-one-identity)).\n\nA tenant holds these objects:\n\n- Applications, also called clients: the apps that sign users in, and the programs that call APIs.\n- APIs, also called resource servers, with their scopes.\n- Client grants, which allow a machine-to-machine application to call an API.\n- Connections: the places where users sign in, such as a user database or a company directory.\n- Roles, organizations, and actions, which are small programs that run during sign-in.\n- Log streams, which send the tenant's logs to another system.\n- Tenant-wide settings, such as branding and the sign-in pages.\n\nThe provider has three families of resources for these objects ([Three Families of Resources](#three-families-of-resources-three-families-of-resources)). The family decides what an import ID looks like and what a destroy does. Three more ideas explain most plans:\n\n- For links between objects, a plural resource owns the whole list, and a singular resource adds one item ([Plural Owns, Singular Adds](#plural-owns-singular-adds-plural-owns-singular-adds)).\n- An action is a draft until it is deployed, and it runs only when it is bound to a flow ([Actions Have Three States](#actions-have-three-states-actions-have-three-states)).\n- The state holds client secrets, action secrets and identity-provider secrets ([State Is a Secrets Store](#state-is-a-secrets-store-state-is-a-secrets-store)).\n\nNow compare Aiven. Both providers are remote controls for a service that someone else runs. In Auth0, however, people also change the same tenant in its dashboard every day, so drift is normal.\n\nThe map has four parts. Mental Models explains the ideas above. Reading an Auth0 Configuration says what you will see and where to look first. Pitfalls lists the failures that experienced users plan around. Good and Bad Decisions compares the main design choices.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "terraform-auth0"
      }
    },
